WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:05.340
If you need some help satisfying your true
crime itch and sifting through all the titles

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.960
online, then grab your magnifying glass and
prepare to do some investigating as we look

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.600
at the very best true crime movies and documentaries
that Netflix has to offer.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.740
In 2007, while on vacation with her family
in Portugal, three-year-old Madeleine McCann

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.540
disappeared from her bed, and has never been
seen or heard from since.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.300
Madeleine's case has resulted in a multitude
of theories and tons of speculation regarding

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.420
who was responsible for her kidnapping.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.080
Was it an unknown predator?

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.460
Were her parents, Kate and Gerry, to blame?

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:41.680
"I think that the feeling here was, they lied
to us, they were lying to all the world."

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.620
There are conspiracy theories galore about
what happened here, and The Disappearance

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:51.170
of Madeleine McCann takes on the enormous
task of examining Madeleine's abduction from

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:56.200
the moment she and her family arrived at their
overseas resort to the ongoing investigation.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.660
Not only does the series offer an in-depth
timeline of events, but it also discusses

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.471
the police investigation, Kate and Gerry McCann's
media campaign to find Madeleine, and the

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.720
potential theories surrounding the abduction.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.600
Sister Cathy Cesnik was a nun and teacher
at Archbishop Keough High School in Baltimore.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.440
Known for her kindness and dependability,
Cesnik was loved by everyone who knew her.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.260
So when she failed to return home one night
in 1969, it was obvious that something was

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:23.760
wrong.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:30.060
Tragically, Cesnik's body was found in early
1970, but for years, the nun's case went unsolved.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.150
That all changed in 1992, when a former Keough
student came forward and claimed that Father

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.880
Joseph Maskell, the school's chaplain, had
sexually abused her.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.500
From there, authorities dove into Father Maskell's
backstory, uncovering a history of abuse and

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.740
a lead in Cesnik's tragic passing.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:52.260
This twisty tale is documented in The Keepers,
a seven-episode series that dives into Cesnik's

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.500
case and the allegations of abuse that occurred
at Archbishop Keough High School.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.700
The series explores the notion that Cesnik's
murder is directly connected to the claims

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.340
of abuse, and the show does an excellent job
untangling the various testimonies, timelines,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.940
and theories associated with the homicide.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.960
The Keepers is thoroughly engaging, but it's
also deeply emotional and difficult to stomach.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.900
It's a story of homicide, abuse, and cover-ups.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.060
But most importantly, it's about a community
of survivors coming together to find justice,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.160
both for Sister Cathy Cesnik and for themselves.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.230
Ted Bundy is one of the most well-known serial
killers in American history, thanks to both

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.700
his brutal crimes and his bizarre behavior
throughout his trial.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.200
His psychopathic insanity has been captured
for the whole world to see in Conversations

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.930
with a Killer: The Ted Bundy Tapes.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.120
"It's hard for me to believe that what I did
is newsworthy."

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.980
Released on the 30th anniversary of Bundy's
execution, The Ted Bundy Tapes recounts the

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.660
man's life, including his crimes, escape attempts,
and eventual execution via the electric chair.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.719
But what separates The Ted Bundy Tapes from
every other movie and TV show about the killer's

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.840
crimes is the never-before-heard audio interviews
with Bundy himself.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.120
"With every murder, leaving a person of this
type hungry.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.140
Unfulfilled."

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:15.000
The Ted Bundy Tapes is a chilling experience
because we know that Bundy is, in fact, responsible

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.960
for the crimes of which he was accused.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.599
Hearing him speak as if he'd done nothing
wrong is both an aggravating and horrifying

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.260
experience.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.820
"The next time he did it, he would be fulfilled.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.940
And the next time he did it he would be fulfilled.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.620
Or the next time he did it he would be fulfilled."

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.440
In each interview, it's clear that Bundy's
manipulative nature is at play, and he's attempting

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.460
to sway listeners with every word.

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:42.260
If you're interested in learning how a maniac
charmed so many people, The Ted Bundy Tapes

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.700
is definitely worth a watch.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:50.319
In December 2015, the documentary series Making
a Murderer was released on Netflix, and almost

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.520
immediately, it became a massive hit.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:56.960
It garnered attention from big names like
Ricky Gervais, Alec Baldwin, and Mandy Moore

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.120
on social media.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.500
The series even inspired viewers to take action
in the form of online petitions.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.380
But why did this documentary impact so many
people?

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.500
The series follows the story of Steven Avery,
a man who served 18 years in prison for the

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.400
attempted killing of Penny Beernsten.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.900
With the help of the Innocence Project, Avery
was released in 2003 after DNA evidence proved

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.080
that he was not responsible for the crime.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.740
However, two years after his release, Avery
was convicted of killing a second woman named

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:25.640
Teresa Halbach.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:30.540
So is Avery guilty of either crime, or is
the man getting railroaded by the system?

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:32.920
"They just don't get what happened.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.420
You know, they don't care about the truth."

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.660
The first season of the series is mostly interested
in trying to prove Avery's innocence, and

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.700
it makes a compelling argument regarding the
handling of his case.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.220
The second season focuses more on Avery's
lawyer as she attempts to prove his innocence

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.220
while having to navigate every legal battle
imaginable.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.560
The controversial show is wildly popular,
and if you're wondering why, now is the time

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:56.420
to find out.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.560
In the 1980s, a controversial Indian guru
named Bhagwan Shree Rajneesh founded the religious

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.320
movement Rajneeshpuram.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:08.100
When the community moved to a large piece
of land in Wasco County, Oregon, with the

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.760
intent of creating their own city, the neighboring
small town of Antelope decided to fight back.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.400
Not only was there resistance from their neighbors,
but Rajneesh and his followers faced constant

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:21.220
legal battles and media backlash that threatened
their entire community.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.060
It would all lead to the largest bioterrorism
attack in US history, an event documented

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.680
in one of Netflix's very best series, Wild
Wild Country.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.220
"Someone is out there, who poisoned an entire
town."

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.540
Wild Wild Country gives a voice to Rajneesh's
followers, as well as the residents of Antelope.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.910
By interviewing members on each side of the
conflict, we get an in-depth view of how the

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:48.889
community came to exist, what life was like
for them, and an examination of the prominent

00:05:48.889 --> 00:05:50.000
figures involved.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:54.360
The series paints a vivid picture of the events
that transpired as the community attempted

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.280
to create and hold onto their utopian commune,
at any cost.

